        <mxCell id="T4ZiRt8O989ueM4nzAIV-30" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(248, 248, 246); font-family: &amp;quot;Anthropic Sans&amp;quot;, system-ui, &amp;quot;Segoe UI&amp;quot;, Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; text-align: start; white-space: pre-wrap; background-color: rgb(18, 18, 18);&quot;&gt;the client has ascertained that she carries enough GIS knowledge to maintain all geoprocessing within arcgis. the sole purpose of the python script is to perform certain preprocessing functions, explicitly calculate county centroid for each specimen that has a &#39;false&#39; georeferencedValidation value, perform a check to see that all entries &#39;county&#39; value matches the county it lies within [geopandas], table join with the tracking list [using strip(), upper(), and other formatting function], and lastly omitting any entries for orchid family and horse crippler species. with all that being said, i would like to utilize geopandas within the script and possibly create a model using modelbuilder that would truncate and append the hosted feature class; that way, she has it as a fall back if she ever needs to republish quickly. does that sound streamlined and efficient?&lt;/span&gt;" vertex="1">
